WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
In this video,

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:05.240
I review the health
benefits of millets,

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.720
including its potential
impact on diabetes.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.935
Check it out.

00:00:09.085 --> 00:00:16.003
"Is Millet Healthy?"

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.240
Millets are highly nutritious
but vastly ignored

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.920
as a main source of food, primarily
due to lack of awareness.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.320
You’ve heard of “ancient grains”?
Millets ain’t messin’ around,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.040
arguably the ﬁrst grains
cultivated by humankind,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.520
not just dating back 5,000 years,
but maybe 10,000 years.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:44.120
Why the plural, millets?
Talk about lack of awareness!

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.000
I had no idea that millet wasn’t
the name of a specific grain.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.200
Millet is just a generic term
that doesn’t just apply to a bunch

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.920
of different species but to a
bunch of totally different plants.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.200
There are so-called major
and minor millets.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.240
There’s pearl millet, which is what
I think most people think of

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.160
as millet, but then there are proso,
foxtail, and finger millets

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.720
which are all completely different grains.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.800
They look similar,
but they’re not the same.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.360
Fiber is one of the main things we’re
looking for in a whole grain,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.160
and kodo millet is like off the charts.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.120
But compared to other grains,
finger and foxtail millet

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.960
also beat out the bunch, though note
that what most people think of

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.760
as millet is really on the low side.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.040
But looking at the polyphenol
content, even plain millet

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.560
beats out the other grains,
including sorghum,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.200
which I previously hyped
for its polyphenol content.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.080
But again, kodo millet
seems to win the day.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.360
Total antioxidant-wise, though, kodo
and finger millet are comparably high.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.440
Nutrition-wise, finger millet is said
to have eight times more calcium

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.440
than other grains, but it looks
to me more like ten times more,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.480
just off the charts, and three
times as much calcium as milk.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.800
Some of the millets are
exceptionally high in iron as well.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.640
Regular millet is high, but barnyard millet

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.800
has like five times more iron than steak.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:21.840
Okay, so it’s nutritious, but what
about specific potential health beneﬁts?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.520
In the medical literature,
you’ll read things like this:

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.240
Millets may prevent cardiovascular
disease by reducing triglycerides

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.520
in hyperlipidemic rats.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.640
Who cares (other than
perhaps pet owners)

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.880
whether a food reduces
cardiovascular disease in rodents?

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.880
There was that epidemiological
study in China that found

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.880
lower esophageal cancer mortality
rates in areas that ate

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.320
more millet and sorghum,
compared to corn and wheat,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.360
but that may have been more
due to avoiding a contaminating

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.040
carcinogenic fungus than from
benefit in the millet itself.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.200
Studies show millets may be effective
against cancer cell proliferation,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.880
in a petri dish, with kodo and
proso millet rapidly inhibiting

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.120
cancer cell growth compared to
pearl or foxtail millet,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.040
knocking down the growth of cancer
cells, but leaving normal cells alone,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.400
reducing the growth of
colon cancer cells

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.640
as well as human breast cancer,
and human liver cancer cells,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.280
potentially also helping
to prevent metastases

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.640
by inhibiting cancer cell migration.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.400
My patients are neither pets nor
petri dishes, though, and to date,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.360
there have been no clinical
cancer trials with millet.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.920
Are there any unique
health-promoting attributes?

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.880
Finger millet is supposedly known
for its health benefits,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.480
such as blood sugar-lowering,
cholesterol-lowering,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.120
and anti-ulcer characteristics.
But the anti-ulcer study they cite

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.560
just noted that some of the areas
with a low incidence of ulcers

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.720
also happened to be eating millet,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.080
but that’s far from establishing
cause-and-effect.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.880
And the cholesterol-
lowering study they cite?

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.840
It explored what happens when
you take tail tendons from rats

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.360
and soak them in sugar
and millet. What?!

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.840
But the blood-sugar lowering
benefits are legit.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.520
Apart from the fact that
millets don’t contain gluten,

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:19.160
which is good for the 1% or 2%
of people with celiac disease

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:23.240
or non-celiac gluten sensitivity,
millets can also be exploited

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.680
in the management of type 2
diabetes due to their blood sugar

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:30.600
lowering properties, as reported
by several studies on millets

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:38.200
and millet-based foods in actual
people, which we’ll cover next.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.440
How does millet come to
the help of diabetics?

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.840
A substantial portion of the starch
in millet is resistant starch,

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.200
meaning resistant to digestion
in our small intestine,

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:54.320
so providing a bounty for
the good bugs in our colon.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.840
Here’s how the various millets do,
all way more than more common

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.160
grains like rice or wheat, but
proso and kodo millet lead the pack.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.240
What’s going on? The protein
matrix in millet not only acts

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.160
as a physical barrier but actually
also partially sequesters

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.400
your starch-munching enzyme,
and the millet polyphenols

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.360
also can act as starch
blockers in and of themselves.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.920
Millet also has markedly slower
stomach emptying times

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.360
than other starchy foods. If you eat
white rice, boiled potatoes,

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.160
or pasta, your stomach takes
about an hour to digest it,

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.800
before starting to slowly
dump it into your intestines,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.000
and two or three hours
to empty about half-way.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.040
Whereas you eat sorghum or
millet, and stomach emptying

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.720
doesn’t even start
until two or three hours

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:50.360
and may take five hours
to empty even halfway.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.800
Note this was for both
a thick millet porridge

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.760
and for just millet couscous.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:02.200
Since the non-viscous millet
couscous meal was also equally slow

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.080
in emptying, this suggests that
there may just be something

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.240
about millet itself that
helps slow stomach emptying,

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.960
which should blunt the
blood sugar spike.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:17.040
But you don’t know...until
you put it to the test.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.680
And indeed, millet caused about a
20% lower surge in blood sugar

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.960
than the same amount of
carbs in the form of rice.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.440
Remember how excited I was to show
you how it only took the body

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.480
like half the insulin to handle
sorghum compared to a grain like corn?

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.040
Well, millet did even better.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:40.240
Give a group of prediabetics only
about three-quarters of a cup

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.200
of millet a day, and within six
weeks their insulin resistance

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.160
dropped so much their
prediabetic fasting blood sugars

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.840
turned into non-
prediabetic blood sugars.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.640
This so-called “self-
controlled” clinical trial,

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:57.520
the same subjects before and
after, is just a sneaky way

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.840
of saying an uncontrolled trial.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.880
There was no control group
that didn’t add the millet

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.640
or added something else.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.680
And we know just being in
a study under scrutiny

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.440
can cause people to eat
better in other ways.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.920
So, we don’t know what role,
if any, the millet itself played.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.960
What we need is a randomized,
controlled crossover trial

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.840
where the same people eat
both a millet-containing

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.480
and non-millet containing diet
and see which works better.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.640
And here we go! A randomized,
crossover study having

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:32.160
hundreds of patients both do an
American Diabetes Association-type

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:36.400
diet with and without about one and
one-third cup of millet every day,

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.400
and… the millet-based diet
lowered hemoglobin A1C levels,

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.480
meaning an improvement in long-term
blood sugar control,

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.840
along with some side benefits
like lowering cholesterol.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.960
The target for good blood
sugar control recommended

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.200
by the American Diabetes Association
is an A1C less than 7.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.680
They started out at 8.37, but
after a few months on millet,

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.320
dropped it to an average of 6.77.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.520
Is it just because they lost
weight or something?

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:11.000
No, suggesting that it was an
effect specific to the millet.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.840
But they didn’t just give millet.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.600
They mixed the millet with
split black lentils and spices,

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.640
and we know from
dozens of randomized,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.920
controlled experimental trials in
people with and without diabetes

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.480
that the consumption of pulses
(meaning beans, split peas,

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.120
chickpeas, or lentils) can
improve long-term measures

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:31.040
of blood sugar control like A1C levels.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.080
So, while the researchers conclude
that millets have the potential

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.240
for a protective role in
the management of diabetes,

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.640
a more accurate conclusion might
be a mix of millet and lentils

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:45.480
can be protective, though hey,
maybe the spices helped too.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.600
They didn’t say which ones they
used, and I couldn’t get ahold

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.480
of the authors, but a similar study
done by one of the same researchers

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.920
included about a tablespoon a day of
a mixture of fenugreek, coriander,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:02.699
cumin, and black pepper, with a fifth
spice, perhaps cinnamon or turmeric.

